␀␀␀¯û␀␀␀␀␀¯û␀␀Úû␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀pOffset the camera's position by this much (camera space)¨û␀␀␀␀0When to apply the offset¨û␀␀␀␀ÄIf applying offset after aim, re-adjust the aim to preserve the screen position of the LookAt target as much as possible¨û␀␀␀␀8When to apply the adjustment¨û␀␀␀␀8Tilt the camera by this much¨û␀␀␀␀6Pan the camera by this much¨û␀␀␀␀8Roll the camera by this much¨û␀␀␀␀TScale the zoom by this amount (normal = 1)¨û␀␀␀␀Ä®Lowering this value relaxes the camera's attention to the Follow target (normal = 1)¨û␀␀␀␀Ä®Lowering this value relaxes the camera's attention to the LookAt target (normal = 1)¨û␀␀␀␀BSensitivity multiplier for x-axis¨û␀␀␀␀BSensitivity multiplier for y-axis¨û␀␀␀␀BInput channel to spoof for X axis¨û␀␀␀␀BInput channel to spoof for Y axis¨û␀␀␀␀PObjects on these layers will be detected¨û␀␀␀␀ÄæObjects with this tag will be ignored.  It is a good idea to set this field to the target's tag¨û␀␀␀␀VHow far to project the object detection ray¨û␀␀␀␀Å∫This 2D object will be positioned in the game view over the raycast hit point, if any, or will remain in the center of the screen if no hit point is detected.  May be null, in which case no on-screen indicator will appear␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÅRDefault object for the camera children to look at (the aim target), if not specified in a child camera.  May be empty if all of the children define targets of their own.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅdDefault object for the camera children wants to move with (the body target), if not specified in a child camera.  May be empty if all of the children define targets of their own.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä»When enabled, the current child camera and blend will be indicated in the game window, for debugging¨û␀␀␀␀ÄºWhen enabled, the child vcams will cycle indefinitely instead of just stopping at the last oneá†␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀fThe set of instructions for enabling child cameras.␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÜThe virtual camera to activate when this instruction becomes active¨û␀␀␀␀Ä∏How long to wait (in seconds) before activating the next virtual camera in the list (if any)¨û␀␀␀␀xHow to blend to the next virtual camera in the list (if any)¨û␀␀␀␀ÄºWhen enabled, the current camera and blend will be indicated in the game window, for debugging¨û␀␀␀␀ÄûWhen enabled, the camera's frustum will be shown at all times in the scene view¨û␀␀␀␀Å␀When enabled, the cameras will always respond in real-time to user input and damping, even if the game is running in slow motion¨û␀␀␀␀Ç\If set, this object's Y axis will define the worldspace Up vector for all the virtual cameras.  This is useful for instance in top-down game environments.  If not set, Up is worldspace Y.  Setting this appropriately is important, because Virtual Cameras don't like looking straight up or straight down.¨û␀␀␀␀ÇäThe update time for the vcams.  Use FixedUpdate if all your targets are animated during FixedUpdate (e.g. RigidBodies), LateUpdate if all your targets are animated during the normal Update loop, and SmartUpdate if you want Cinemachine to do the appropriate thing on a per-target basis.  SmartUpdate is the recommended setting¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÃThe update time for the Brain, i.e. when the blends are evaluated and the brain's transform is updated¨û␀␀␀␀Ä–The blend that is used in cases where you haven't explicitly defined a blend between two Virtual Cameras¨û␀␀␀␀Ä“This is the asset that contains custom settings for blends between specific virtual cameras in your scene¨û␀␀␀␀ÄúThis event will fire whenever a virtual camera goes live and there is no blend¨û␀␀␀␀ÅThis event will fire whenever a virtual camera goes live.  If a blend is involved, then the event will fire on the first frame of the blend.␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÅFDefault object for the camera children to look at (the aim target), if not specified in a child camera.  May be empty if all children specify targets of their own.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅXDefault object for the camera children wants to move with (the body target), if not specified in a child camera.  May be empty if all children specify targets of their own.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä»When enabled, the current child camera and blend will be indicated in the game window, for debuggingá†␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀vWait this many seconds before activating a new child camera¨û␀␀␀␀|An active camera must be active for at least this many seconds¨û␀␀␀␀Å8If checked, camera choice will be randomized if multiple cameras are equally desirable.  Otherwise, child list order and child camera priority will be used.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¥The blend which is used if you don't explicitly define a blend between two Virtual Cameras␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀PObjects on these layers will be detected¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¬Obstacles with this tag will be ignored.  It is a good idea to set this field to the target's tag¨û␀␀␀␀|Objects on these layers will never obstruct view of the target¨û␀␀␀␀pObstacles closer to the target than this will be ignored¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀Ä‡When enabled, will attempt to resolve situations where the line of sight to the target is blocked by an obstacle␀␀*m_PreserveLineOfSight¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀ÅfThe maximum raycast distance when checking if the line of sight to this camera's target is clear.  If the setting is 0 or less, the current actual distance to target will be used.␀␀6m_LineOfSightFeelerDistance¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÇDon't take action unless occlusion has lasted at least this long.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅZCamera will try to maintain this distance from any obstacle.  Try to keep this value small.  Increase it if you are seeing inside obstacles due to a large FOV on the camera.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄñThe way in which the Collider will attempt to preserve sight of the target.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä˙Upper limit on how many obstacle hits to process.  Higher numbers may impact performance.  In most environments, 4 is enough.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄƒSmoothing to apply to obstruction resolution.  Nearest camera point is held for at least this long¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀Å0How gradually the camera returns to its normal position after having been corrected.  Higher numbers will move the camera more gradually back to normal.␀␀m_Smoothing¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÿHow gradually the camera moves to resolve an occlusion.  Higher numbers will move the camera more gradually.¨û␀␀␀␀Å$If greater than zero, a higher score will be given to shots when the target is closer to this distance.  Set this to zero to disable this feature.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄîThe confiner can operate using a 2D bounding shape or a 3D bounding volume¨û␀␀␀␀jThe volume within which the camera is to be contained¨û␀␀␀␀nThe 2D shape within which the camera is to be contained¨û␀␀␀␀Å
If camera is orthographic, screen edges will be confined to the volume.  If not checked, then only the camera center will be confined¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÙHow gradually to return the camera to the bounding volume if it goes beyond the borders.  Higher numbers are more gradual.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄŒThe 2D shape within which the camera is to be contained.  Can be a 2D polygon or 2D composite collider.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä†Damping applied around corners to avoid jumps.  Higher numbers are more gradual.¨û␀␀␀␀ÉàTo optimize computation and memory costs, set this to the largest view size that the camera is expected to have.  The confiner will not compute a polygon cache for frustum sizes larger than this.  This refers to the size in world units of the frustum at the confiner plane (for orthographic cameras, this is just the orthographic size).  If set to 0, then this parameter is ignored and a polygon cache will be calculated for all potential window sizes.¨û␀␀␀␀Å∂For large window sizes, the confiner will potentially generate polygons with zero area.  The padding may be used to add a small amount of area to these polygons, to prevent them from being a series of disconnected dots.¨û␀␀␀␀$The path to follow¨û␀␀␀␀\When to move the cart, if Velocity is non-zero¨û␀␀␀␀ÇTHow to interpret the Path Position.  If set to Path Units, values are as follows: 0 represents the first waypoint on the path, 1 is the second, and so on.  Values in-between are points on the path in between the waypoints.  If set to Distance, then Path Position represents distance along the path.¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀Ä‡Move the cart with this speed along the path.  The value is interpreted according to the Position Units setting.␀␀m_Velocity¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀ÅæThe position along the path at which the cart will be placed.  This can be animated directly or, if the velocity is non-zero, will be updated automatically.  The value is interpreted according to the Position Units setting.␀␀"m_CurrentDistance¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÏThe object that the camera is looking at.  Setting this will improve the quality of the blends to and from this camera␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀vHint for blending positions to and from this virtual camera␀␀$m_PositionBlending¨û␀␀␀␀~The shot width to maintain, in world units, at target distance.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅFIncrease this value to soften the aggressiveness of the follow-zoom.  Small numbers are more responsive, larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera.¨û␀␀␀␀tLower limit for the FOV that this behaviour will generate.¨û␀␀␀␀tUpper limit for the FOV that this behaviour will generate.¨û␀␀␀␀vObject for the camera children to look at (the aim target).¨û␀␀␀␀ÄàObject for the camera children wants to move with (the body target).¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀ÄËIf enabled, this lens setting will apply to all three child rigs, otherwise the child rig lens settings will be used␀␀,m_UseCommonLensSetting'¢␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ m_LensAttributes␀␀ÅdSpecifies the lens properties of this Virtual Camera.  This generally mirrors the Unity Camera's lens settings, and will be used to drive the Unity camera when the vcam is activeá†␀␀'¢␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀␀␀␀m_BlendHint␀␀$m_PositionBlending¨û␀␀␀␀ÄéThe Vertical axis.  Value is 0..1.  Chooses how to blend the child rigs¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÄControls how automatic recentering of the Y axis is accomplished¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÃThe Horizontal axis.  Value is -180...180.  This is passed on to the rigs' OrbitalTransposer component¨û␀␀␀␀lThe definition of Forward.  Camera will follow behind.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÄControls how automatic recentering of the X axis is accomplished¨û␀␀␀␀ÅXThe coordinate space to use when interpreting the offset from the target.  This is also used to set the camera's Up vector, which will be maintained when aiming the camera.¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀ÄÿControls how taut is the line that connects the rigs' orbits, which determines final placement on the Y axis␀␀m_SplineTension¨û␀␀␀␀bThe radius and height of the three orbiting rigs.á†␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀␀␀␀m_HeadingBiasá†␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀LThe weight of the first tracked camera¨û␀␀␀␀NThe weight of the second tracked camera¨û␀␀␀␀LThe weight of the third tracked camera¨û␀␀␀␀NThe weight of the fourth tracked camera¨û␀␀␀␀LThe weight of the fifth tracked camera¨û␀␀␀␀LThe weight of the sixth tracked camera¨û␀␀␀␀PThe weight of the seventh tracked camera¨û␀␀␀␀NThe weight of the eighth tracked camera␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÄàIf checked, then the path ends are joined to form a continuous loop.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä®The waypoints that define the path.  They will be interpolated using a bezier curve.¨û␀␀␀␀8Position in path-local space¨û␀␀␀␀ÅÊOffset from the position, which defines the tangent of the curve at the waypoint.  The length of the tangent encodes the strength of the bezier handle.  The same handle is used symmetrically on both sides of the waypoint, to ensure smoothness.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÏDefines the roll of the path at this waypoint.  The other orientation axes are inferred from the tangent and world up.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄàIf checked, then the path ends are joined to form a continuous loop.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä®The waypoints that define the path.  They will be interpolated using a bezier curve.¨û␀␀␀␀8Position in path-local space¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÏDefines the roll of the path at this waypoint.  The other orientation axes are inferred from the tangent and world up.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅRDefault object for the camera children to look at (the aim target), if not specified in a child camera.  May be empty if all of the children define targets of their own.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅdDefault object for the camera children wants to move with (the body target), if not specified in a child camera.  May be empty if all of the children define targets of their own.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä™The state machine whose state changes will drive this camera's choice of active child¨û␀␀␀␀dWhich layer in the target state machine to observe¨û␀␀␀␀Ä»When enabled, the current child camera and blend will be indicated in the game window, for debuggingá†␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÅThe set of instructions associating virtual cameras with states.  These instructions are used to choose the live child at any given moment¨û␀␀␀␀ÄƒThe blend which is used if you don't explicitly define a blend between two Virtual Camera children¨û␀␀␀␀ÄîThis is the asset which contains custom settings for specific child blendsá†␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀HThe full hash of the animation state¨û␀␀␀␀ÄåThe virtual camera to activate when the animation state becomes active¨û␀␀␀␀ÄﬁHow long to wait (in seconds) before activating the virtual camera. This filters out very short state durations¨û␀␀␀␀ÄéThe minimum length of time (in seconds) to keep a virtual camera active¨û␀␀␀␀\If checked, all storyboards are globally muted¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¿If checked, the specified image will be displayed as an overlay over the virtual camera's output¨û␀␀␀␀(The image to display¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÄHow to handle differences between image aspect and screen aspect¨û␀␀␀␀pThe opacity of the image.  0 is transparent, 1 is opaque¨û␀␀␀␀ÄêThe screen-space position at which to display the image.  Zero is center¨û␀␀␀␀^The screen-space rotation to apply to the image¨û␀␀␀␀\The screen-space scaling to apply to the image¨û␀␀␀␀TIf checked, X and Y scale are synchronized¨û␀␀␀␀ÄîIf checked, Camera transform will not be controlled by this virtual camera¨û␀␀␀␀LWipe the image on and off horizontally¨û␀␀␀␀~The render mode of the canvas on which the storyboard is drawn.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÑAllows ordering canvases to render on top or below other canvases.¨û␀␀␀␀jHow far away from the camera is the Canvas generated.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅPHow the group's position is calculated.  Select GroupCenter for the center of the bounding box, and GroupAverage for a weighted average of the positions of the members.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅZHow the group's rotation is calculated.  Select Manual to use the value in the group's transform, and GroupAverage for a weighted average of the orientations of the members.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄûWhen to update the group's transform based on the position of the group members¨û␀␀␀␀ÅThe target objects, together with their weights and radii, that will contribute to the group's average position, orientation, and size.¨û␀␀␀␀Å4The target objects.  This object's position and orientation will contribute to the group's average position and orientation, in accordance with its weight¨û␀␀␀␀ÄåHow much weight to give the target when averaging.  Cannot be negative¨û␀␀␀␀Ä®The radius of the target, used for calculating the bounding box.  Cannot be negative¨û␀␀␀␀Å6The object that the camera wants to look at (the Aim target).  If this is null, then the vcam's Transform orientation will define the camera's orientation.¨û␀␀␀␀Å0The object that the camera wants to move with (the Body target).  If this is null, then the vcam's Transform position will define the camera's position.'¢␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ m_LensAttributes␀␀ÅfSpecifies the lens properties of this Virtual Camera.  This generally mirrors the Unity Camera's lens settings, and will be used to drive the Unity camera when the vcam is active.á†␀␀'¢␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀␀␀␀m_BlendHint␀␀$m_PositionBlendingá†␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÇHow responsively the camera tracks the target.  Each axis (camera-local) can have its own setting.  Value is the approximate time it takes the camera to catch up to the target's new position.  Smaller values give a more rigid effect, larger values give a squishier one¨û␀␀␀␀Ä‘Position of the shoulder pivot relative to the Follow target origin.  This offset is in target-local space¨û␀␀␀␀Å.Vertical offset of the hand in relation to the shoulder.  Arm length will affect the follow target's screen position when the camera rotates vertically¨û␀␀␀␀ÄåSpecifies which shoulder (left, right, or in-between) the camera is on¨û␀␀␀␀bHow far behind the hand the camera will be placed¨û␀␀␀␀VCamera will avoid obstacles on these layers¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¬Obstacles with this tag will be ignored.  It is a good idea to set this field to the target's tag¨û␀␀␀␀fSpecifies how close the camera can get to obstacles¨û␀␀␀␀Ä‹How gradually the camera moves to correct for occlusions.  Higher numbers will move the camera more gradually.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅàHow gradually the camera returns to its normal position after having been corrected by the built-in collision resolution system.  Higher numbers will move the camera more gradually back to normal.¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀Å`The asset containing the Noise Profile.  Define the frequencies and amplitudes there to make a characteristic noise profile.  Make your own or just use one of the many presets.␀␀m_Definition¨û␀␀␀␀Ä∞When rotating the camera, offset the camera's pivot position by this much (camera space)¨û␀␀␀␀Å␀Gain to apply to the amplitudes defined in the NoiseSettings asset.  1 is normal.  Setting this to 0 completely mutes the noise.¨û␀␀␀␀Å*Scale factor to apply to the frequencies defined in the NoiseSettings asset.  1 is normal.  Larger magnitudes will make the noise shake more rapidly.á†␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÅbTarget offset from the target object's center in target-local space. Use this to fine-tune the tracking target position when the desired area is not the tracked object's center.¨û␀␀␀␀ÉöThis setting will instruct the composer to adjust its target offset based on the motion of the target.  The composer will look at a point where it estimates the target will be this many seconds into the future.  Note that this setting is sensitive to noisy animation, and can amplify the noise, resulting in undesirable camera jitter.  If the camera jitters unacceptably when the target is in motion, turn down this setting, or animate the target more smoothly.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅControls the smoothness of the lookahead algorithm.  Larger values smooth out jittery predictions and also increase prediction lag¨û␀␀␀␀Ä†If checked, movement along the Y axis will be ignored for lookahead calculations¨û␀␀␀␀ÇÆHow aggressively the camera tries to follow the target in the screen-horizontal direction. Small numbers are more responsive, rapidly orienting the camera to keep the target in the dead zone. Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera. Using different vertical and horizontal settings can yield a wide range of camera behaviors.¨û␀␀␀␀Ç™How aggressively the camera tries to follow the target in the screen-vertical direction. Small numbers are more responsive, rapidly orienting the camera to keep the target in the dead zone. Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera. Using different vertical and horizontal settings can yield a wide range of camera behaviors.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄƒHorizontal screen position for target. The camera will rotate to position the tracked object here.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¿Vertical screen position for target, The camera will rotate to position the tracked object here.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÆCamera will not rotate horizontally if the target is within this range of the position.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä™Camera will not rotate vertically if the target is within this range of the position.¨û␀␀␀␀Å,When target is within this region, camera will gradually rotate horizontally to re-align towards the desired position, depending on the damping speed.¨û␀␀␀␀Å(When target is within this region, camera will gradually rotate vertically to re-align towards the desired position, depending on the damping speed.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¬A non-zero bias will move the target position horizontally away from the center of the soft zone.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄæA non-zero bias will move the target position vertically away from the center of the soft zone.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÓForce target to center of screen when this camera activates.  If false, will clamp target to the edges of the dead zone␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÇOffset from the Follow Target object (in target-local co-ordinates).  The camera will attempt to frame the point which is the target's position plus this offset.  Use it to correct for cases when the target's origin is not the point of interest for the camera.¨û␀␀␀␀ÉöThis setting will instruct the composer to adjust its target offset based on the motion of the target.  The composer will look at a point where it estimates the target will be this many seconds into the future.  Note that this setting is sensitive to noisy animation, and can amplify the noise, resulting in undesirable camera jitter.  If the camera jitters unacceptably when the target is in motion, turn down this setting, or animate the target more smoothly.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅControls the smoothness of the lookahead algorithm.  Larger values smooth out jittery predictions and also increase prediction lag¨û␀␀␀␀Ä†If checked, movement along the Y axis will be ignored for lookahead calculations¨û␀␀␀␀ÇrHow aggressively the camera tries to maintain the offset in the X-axis.  Small numbers are more responsive, rapidly translating the camera to keep the target's x-axis offset.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera.  Using different settings per axis can yield a wide range of camera behaviors.¨û␀␀␀␀ÇrHow aggressively the camera tries to maintain the offset in the Y-axis.  Small numbers are more responsive, rapidly translating the camera to keep the target's y-axis offset.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera.  Using different settings per axis can yield a wide range of camera behaviors.¨û␀␀␀␀ÇrHow aggressively the camera tries to maintain the offset in the Z-axis.  Small numbers are more responsive, rapidly translating the camera to keep the target's z-axis offset.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera.  Using different settings per axis can yield a wide range of camera behaviors.¨û␀␀␀␀Å8If set, damping will apply  only to target motion, but not to camera rotation changes.  Turn this on to get an instant response when the rotation changes.  ¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¿Horizontal screen position for target. The camera will move to position the tracked object here.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄºVertical screen position for target, The camera will move to position the tracked object here.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¢The distance along the camera axis that will be maintained from the Follow target¨û␀␀␀␀Ä™Camera will not move horizontally if the target is within this range of the position.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¶Camera will not move vertically if the target is within this range of the position.¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀ÄÓThe camera will not move along its z-axis if the Follow target is within this distance of the specified camera distance␀␀,m_DistanceDeadZoneSize¨û␀␀␀␀tIf checked, then then soft zone will be unlimited in size.¨û␀␀␀␀Å(When target is within this region, camera will gradually move horizontally to re-align towards the desired position, depending on the damping speed.¨û␀␀␀␀Å$When target is within this region, camera will gradually move vertically to re-align towards the desired position, depending on the damping speed.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¬A non-zero bias will move the target position horizontally away from the center of the soft zone.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄæA non-zero bias will move the target position vertically away from the center of the soft zone.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÓForce target to center of screen when this camera activates.  If false, will clamp target to the edges of the dead zone¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀Ä¨What screen dimensions to consider when framing.  Can be Horizontal, Vertical, or both␀␀m_FramingMode¨û␀␀␀␀Ä∫How to adjust the camera to get the desired framing.  You can zoom, dolly in/out, or do both.¨û␀␀␀␀Å(The bounding box of the targets should occupy this amount of the screen space.  1 means fill the whole screen.  0.5 means fill half the screen, etc.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä≤The maximum distance toward the target that this behaviour is allowed to move the camera.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÆThe maximum distance away the target that this behaviour is allowed to move the camera.¨û␀␀␀␀zSet this to limit how close to the target the camera can get.¨û␀␀␀␀zSet this to limit how far from the target the camera can get.¨û␀␀␀␀nIf adjusting FOV, will not set the FOV lower than this.¨û␀␀␀␀pIf adjusting FOV, will not set the FOV higher than this.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÄIf adjusting Orthographic Size, will not set it lower than this.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÇIf adjusting Orthographic Size, will not set it higher than this.␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀Å(The bounding box of the targets should occupy this amount of the screen space.  1 means fill the whole screen.  0.5 means fill half the screen, etc.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¨What screen dimensions to consider when framing.  Can be Horizontal, Vertical, or both¨û␀␀␀␀Å¢How aggressively the camera tries to frame the group. Small numbers are more responsive, rapidly adjusting the camera to keep the group in the frame.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä∫How to adjust the camera to get the desired framing.  You can zoom, dolly in/out, or do both.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä≤The maximum distance toward the target that this behaviour is allowed to move the camera.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÆThe maximum distance away the target that this behaviour is allowed to move the camera.¨û␀␀␀␀zSet this to limit how close to the target the camera can get.¨û␀␀␀␀zSet this to limit how far from the target the camera can get.¨û␀␀␀␀nIf adjusting FOV, will not set the FOV lower than this.¨û␀␀␀␀pIf adjusting FOV, will not set the FOV higher than this.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÄIf adjusting Orthographic Size, will not set it lower than this.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÇIf adjusting Orthographic Size, will not set it higher than this.␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÄòHow much time it takes for the position to catch up to the target's position¨û␀␀␀␀lThe definition of Forward.  Camera will follow behind.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä˛Automatic heading recentering.  The settings here defines how the camera will reposition itself in the absence of player input.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä‘Heading Control.  The settings here control the behaviour of the camera in response to the player's input.á†␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀␀␀␀m_Radiusá†␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀␀␀␀m_HeightOffsetá†␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀␀␀␀m_HeadingBias'¢␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀&m_HeadingDefinition␀␀ÅXHow 'forward' is defined.  The camera will be placed by default behind the target.  PositionDelta will consider 'forward' to be the direction in which the target is moving.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅòSize of the velocity sampling window for target heading filter.  This filters out irregularities in the target's movement.  Used only if deriving heading from target's movement (PositionDelta or Velocity)'¢␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀m_HeadingBias␀␀Å‚Where the camera is placed when the X-axis value is zero.  This is a rotation in degrees around the Y axis.  When this value is 0, the camera will be placed behind the target.  Nonzero offsets will rotate the zero position around the target.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄéThe Vertical axis.  Value is -90..90. Controls the vertical orientation¨û␀␀␀␀ÄéControls how automatic recentering of the Vertical axis is accomplished¨û␀␀␀␀ÄúThe Horizontal axis.  Value is -180..180.  Controls the horizontal orientation¨û␀␀␀␀ÄíControls how automatic recentering of the Horizontal axis is accomplished¨û␀␀␀␀2Obsolete - no longer used¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀ÄéHow much time it takes for the aim to catch up to the target's rotation␀␀ m_AngularDamping¨û␀␀␀␀ÄíThe path to which the camera will be constrained.  This must be non-null.¨û␀␀␀␀ÇThe position along the path at which the camera will be placed.  This can be animated directly, or set automatically by the Auto-Dolly feature to get as close as possible to the Follow target.  The value is interpreted according to the Position Units setting.¨û␀␀␀␀ÇLHow to interpret Path Position.  If set to Path Units, values are as follows: 0 represents the first waypoint on the path, 1 is the second, and so on.  Values in-between are points on the path in between the waypoints.  If set to Distance, then Path Position represents distance along the path.¨û␀␀␀␀Å∂Where to put the camera relative to the path position.  X is perpendicular to the path, Y is up, and Z is parallel to the path.  This allows the camera to be offset from the path itself (as if on a tripod, for example).¨û␀␀␀␀Ç™How aggressively the camera tries to maintain its position in a direction perpendicular to the path.  Small numbers are more responsive, rapidly translating the camera to keep the target's x-axis offset.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera. Using different settings per axis can yield a wide range of camera behaviors.¨û␀␀␀␀ÇñHow aggressively the camera tries to maintain its position in the path-local up direction.  Small numbers are more responsive, rapidly translating the camera to keep the target's y-axis offset.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera. Using different settings per axis can yield a wide range of camera behaviors.¨û␀␀␀␀Ç†How aggressively the camera tries to maintain its position in a direction parallel to the path.  Small numbers are more responsive, rapidly translating the camera to keep the target's z-axis offset.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera. Using different settings per axis can yield a wide range of camera behaviors.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅFHow to set the virtual camera's Up vector.  This will affect the screen composition, because the camera Aim behaviours will always try to respect the Up direction.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅRHow aggressively the camera tries to track the target rotation's X angle.  Small numbers are more responsive.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅRHow aggressively the camera tries to track the target rotation's Y angle.  Small numbers are more responsive.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅRHow aggressively the camera tries to track the target rotation's Z angle.  Small numbers are more responsive.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¥Controls how automatic dollying occurs.  A Follow target is necessary to use this feature.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅZIf checked, will enable automatic dolly, which chooses a path position that is as close as possible to the Follow target.  Note: this can have significant performance impact¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¥Offset, in current position units, from the closest point on the path to the follow target¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¿Search up to this many waypoints on either side of the current position.  Use 0 for Entire path.'¢␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀"m_StepsPerSegment␀␀ÅàWe search between waypoints by dividing the segment into this many straight pieces.  he higher the number, the more accurate the result, but performance is proportionally slower for higher numbers¨û␀␀␀␀ÅXThe coordinate space to use when interpreting the offset from the target.  This is also used to set the camera's Up vector, which will be maintained when aiming the camera.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÆThe distance vector that the transposer will attempt to maintain from the Follow target¨û␀␀␀␀ÇpHow aggressively the camera tries to maintain the offset in the X-axis.  Small numbers are more responsive, rapidly translating the camera to keep the target's x-axis offset.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera. Using different settings per axis can yield a wide range of camera behaviors.¨û␀␀␀␀ÇpHow aggressively the camera tries to maintain the offset in the Y-axis.  Small numbers are more responsive, rapidly translating the camera to keep the target's y-axis offset.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera. Using different settings per axis can yield a wide range of camera behaviors.¨û␀␀␀␀ÇpHow aggressively the camera tries to maintain the offset in the Z-axis.  Small numbers are more responsive, rapidly translating the camera to keep the target's z-axis offset.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera. Using different settings per axis can yield a wide range of camera behaviors.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅRHow aggressively the camera tries to track the target rotation's X angle.  Small numbers are more responsive.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅRHow aggressively the camera tries to track the target rotation's Y angle.  Small numbers are more responsive.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅRHow aggressively the camera tries to track the target rotation's Z angle.  Small numbers are more responsive.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅHHow aggressively the camera tries to track the target's orientation.  Small numbers are more responsive.  Larger numbers give a more heavy slowly responding camera.␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀<The current value of the axis.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄºHow to interpret the Max Speed setting: in units/second, or as a direct input value multiplier¨û␀␀␀␀Ä‘The maximum speed of this axis in units/second, or the input value multiplier, depending on the Speed Mode¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÿThe amount of time in seconds it takes to accelerate to MaxSpeed with the supplied Axis at its maximum value¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÊThe amount of time in seconds it takes to decelerate the axis to zero if the supplied axis is in a neutral position'¢␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀m_AxisName␀␀ÅThe name of this axis as specified in Unity Input manager. Setting to an empty string will disable the automatic updating of this axis¨û␀␀␀␀ÅêThe value of the input axis.  A value of 0 means no input.  You can drive this directly from a custom input system, or you can set the Axis Name and have the value driven by the internal Input Manager'¢␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀m_InvertAxis␀␀Ä¶If checked, then the raw value of the input axis will be inverted before it is used¨û␀␀␀␀<The minimum value for the axis¨û␀␀␀␀<The maximum value for the axis¨û␀␀␀␀Ä†If checked, then the axis will wrap around at the min/max values, forming a loop¨û␀␀␀␀RAutomatic recentering to at-rest position␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÄæIf checked, will enable automatic recentering of the axis. If unchecked, recenting is disabled.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä÷If no user input has been detected on the axis, the axis will wait this long in seconds before recentering.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄàHow long it takes to reach destination once recentering has started.á†␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀␀␀␀&m_HeadingDefinitioná†␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀␀␀␀0m_VelocityFilterStrength␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀0Shape of the blend curve¨û␀␀␀␀BDuration of the blend, in seconds␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÄîThe array containing explicitly defined blends between two Virtual Cameras¨û␀␀␀␀<When blending from this camera¨û␀␀␀␀8When blending to this camera¨û␀␀␀␀,Blend curve definition¨û␀␀␀␀ÄêPath samples per waypoint.  This is used for calculating path distances.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄòThe settings that control how the path will appear in the editor scene view.¨û␀␀␀␀xThe color of the path itself when it is active in the editor¨û␀␀␀␀|The color of the path itself when it is inactive in the editor¨û␀␀␀␀ÄäThe width of the railroad-tracks that are drawn to represent the pathá†␀␀␀␀␀á†␀␀␀␀␀á†␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀Å&The priority will determine which camera becomes active based on the state of other cameras and this camera.  Higher numbers have greater priority.¨û␀␀␀␀Å¢When the virtual camera is not live, this is how often the virtual camera will be updated.  Set this to tune for performance. Most of the time Never is fine, unless the virtual camera is doing shot evaluation.␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀vHint for blending positions to and from this virtual camera␀␀$m_PositionBlending¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¯When this virtual camera goes Live, attempt to force the position to be the same as the current position of the Unity Camera¨û␀␀␀␀dThis event fires when the virtual camera goes Live␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÇîThis is the camera view in degrees. Display will be in vertical degress, unless the associated camera has its FOV axis setting set to Horizontal, in which case display will be in horizontal degress.  Internally, it is always vertical degrees.  For cinematic people, a 50mm lens on a super-35mm sensor would equal a 19.6 degree FOV¨û␀␀␀␀Ä‘When using an orthographic camera, this defines the half-height, in world coordinates, of the camera view.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅÊThis defines the near region in the renderable range of the camera frustum. Raising this value will stop the game from drawing things near the camera, which can sometimes come in handy.  Larger values will also increase your shadow resolution.¨û␀␀␀␀Å\This defines the far region of the renderable range of the camera frustum. Typically you want to set this value as low as possible without cutting off desired distant objects¨û␀␀␀␀FCamera Z roll, or tilt, in degrees.¨û␀␀␀␀Å‰Allows you to select a different camera mode to apply to the Camera component when Cinemachine activates this Virtual Camera.  The changes applied to the Camera component through this setting will remain after the Virtual Camera deactivation.á†␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀ÅTThese are the noise channels for the virtual camera's position. Convincing noise setups typically mix low, medium and high frequencies together, so start with a size of 3␀␀m_Position¨û␀␀'¢␀␀␀␀ÅZThese are the noise channels for the virtual camera's orientation. Convincing noise setups typically mix low, medium and high frequencies together, so start with a size of 3␀␀m_Orientation¨û␀␀␀␀ÄñThe frequency of noise for this channel.  Higher magnitudes vibrate faster.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄòThe amplitude of the noise for this channel.  Larger numbers vibrate higher.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄàIf checked, then the amplitude and frequency will not be randomized.¨û␀␀␀␀6Noise definition for X-axis¨û␀␀␀␀6Noise definition for Y-axis¨û␀␀␀␀6Noise definition for Z-axis␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÄäOnly triggers generated by objects on these layers will be considered¨û␀␀␀␀ÄñIf set, only triggers generated by objects with this tag will be considered¨û␀␀␀␀vTriggers generated by objects with this tag will be ignored¨û␀␀␀␀fSkip this many trigger entries before taking action¨û␀␀␀␀hRepeat the action for all subsequent trigger entries¨û␀␀␀␀&What action to take¨û␀␀␀␀Ä∆The target object on which to operate.  If null, then the current behaviour/GameObject will be used¨û␀␀␀␀ÄòIf PriorityBoost, this amount will be added to the virtual camera's priority¨û␀␀␀␀RIf playing a timeline, start at this time¨û␀␀␀␀>How to interpret the start time¨û␀␀␀␀4This event will be invoked¨û␀␀␀␀RThe weight of the group member at index 0¨û␀␀␀␀RThe weight of the group member at index 1¨û␀␀␀␀RThe weight of the group member at index 2¨û␀␀␀␀RThe weight of the group member at index 3¨û␀␀␀␀RThe weight of the group member at index 4¨û␀␀␀␀RThe weight of the group member at index 5¨û␀␀␀␀RThe weight of the group member at index 6¨û␀␀␀␀RThe weight of the group member at index 7¨û␀␀␀␀ÄíOnly collisions with objects on these layers will generate Impulse events¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¢No Impulse evemts will be generated for collisions with objects having these tags¨û␀␀␀␀ÄêIf checked, signal direction will be affected by the direction of impact¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¶If checked, signal amplitude will be multiplied by the mass of the impacting object¨û␀␀␀␀Ä®If checked, signal amplitude will be multiplied by the speed of the impacting object¨û␀␀␀␀JThe raw signal shape along the X axis¨û␀␀␀␀JThe raw signal shape along the Y axis¨û␀␀␀␀JThe raw signal shape along the Z axis¨û␀␀␀␀ÄûImpulse events generated here will appear on the channels included in the mask.¨û␀␀␀␀4Shape of the impact signal¨û␀␀␀␀ÄäDefines the custom shape of the impact signal that will be generated.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä‘The time during which the impact signal will occur.  The signal shape will be stretched to fill that time.¨û␀␀␀␀^How the impulse travels through space and time.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¸This defines how the widely signal will spread within the effect radius before dissipating with distance from the impact point¨û␀␀␀␀xLegacy mode only: Defines the signal that will be generated.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅLegacy mode only: Gain to apply to the amplitudes defined in the signal source.  1 is normal.  Setting this to 0 completely mutes the signal.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅLegacy mode only: Scale factor to apply to the time axis.  1 is normal.  Larger magnitudes will make the signal progress more rapidly.¨û␀␀␀␀|Legacy mode only: How to fit the signal into the envelope time¨û␀␀␀␀bLegacy mode only: Randomize the signal start time¨û␀␀␀␀ÄˆLegacy mode only: This defines the time-envelope of the signal.  The raw signal will be time-scaled to fit in the envelope.¨û␀␀␀␀Å Legacy mode only: The signal will have full amplitude in this radius surrounding the impact point.  Beyond that it will dissipate with distance.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄºLegacy mode only: How the signal direction behaves as the listener moves away from the origin.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä»Legacy mode only: This defines how the signal will dissipate with distance beyond the impact radius.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä†The signal will have no effect outside this radius surrounding the impact point.¨û␀␀␀␀Å¥The speed (m/s) at which the impulse propagates through space.  High speeds allow listeners to react instantaneously, while slower speeds allow listeners in the scene to react as if to a wave spreading from the source.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅWhen to apply the impulse reaction.  Default is after the Noise stage.  Modify this if necessary to influence the ordering of extension effects¨û␀␀␀␀ÄàImpulse events on channels not included in the mask will be ignored.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä÷Gain to apply to the Impulse signal.  1 is normal strength.  Setting this to 0 completely mutes the signal.¨û␀␀␀␀xEnable this to perform distance calculation in 2D (ignore Z)¨û␀␀␀␀tEnable this to process all impulse signals in camera space¨û␀␀␀␀Ç∞This controls the secondary reaction of the listener to the incoming impulse.  The impulse might be for example a sharp shock, and the secondary reaction could be a vibration whose amplitude and duration is controlled by the size of the original impulse.  This allows different listeners to respond in different ways to the same impulse signal.¨û␀␀␀␀|Secondary shake that will be triggered by the primary impulse.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄˆGain to apply to the amplitudes defined in the signal source.  1 is normal.  Setting this to 0 completely mutes the signal.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄËScale factor to apply to the time axis.  1 is normal.  Larger magnitudes will make the signal progress more rapidly.¨û␀␀␀␀LHow long the secondary reaction lasts.á†␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀Ä–Normalized curve defining the shape of the start of the envelope.  If blank a default curve will be used¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÃNormalized curve defining the shape of the end of the envelope.  If blank a default curve will be used¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¨Duration in seconds of the attack.  Attack curve will be scaled to fit.  Must be >= 0.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä®Duration in seconds of the central fully-scaled part of the envelope.  Must be >= 0.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä®Duration in seconds of the decay.  Decay curve will be scaled to fit.  Must be >= 0.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅIf checked, signal amplitude scaling will also be applied to the time envelope of the signal.  Stronger signals will last longer.¨û␀␀␀␀FIf true, then duration is infinite.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅtThe default direction and force of the Impulse Signal in the absense of any specified overrides.  Overrides can be specified by calling the appropriate GenerateImpulse method in the API.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄàImpulse events on channels not included in the mask will be ignored.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä÷Gain to apply to the Impulse signal.  1 is normal strength.  Setting this to 0 completely mutes the signal.¨û␀␀␀␀xEnable this to perform distance calculation in 2D (ignore Z)¨û␀␀␀␀tEnable this to process all impulse signals in camera space¨û␀␀␀␀Ç∞This controls the secondary reaction of the listener to the incoming impulse.  The impulse might be for example a sharp shock, and the secondary reaction could be a vibration whose amplitude and duration is controlled by the size of the original impulse.  This allows different listeners to respond in different ways to the same impulse signal.␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀Å~If the profile has the appropriate overrides, will set the base focus distance to be the distance from the selected target to the camera.The Focus Offset field will then modify that distance.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÄThe target to use if Focus Tracks Target is set to Custom Target¨û␀␀␀␀ÅOffset from target distance, to be used with Focus Tracks Target.  Offsets the sharpest point away from the location of the focus target.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¢This Post-Processing profile will be applied whenever this virtual camera is live␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀l!␀␀␀␀␀l!␀␀␀␀␀l!␀␀␀␀␀l!␀␀␀␀␀l!␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Ùü␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Ùü␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Ùü␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Ùü␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Ùü␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀q␀␀q␀␀␀␀␀␀␀8com.unity.cinemachine.editor␀␀2Cinemachine.Runtime.Tests␀␀␀␀␀Ûû␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀é4␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Úû␀␀Ûû␀␀¯û␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀é4␀␀␀Ûû␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀_≤␀␀␀␀␀Ûû␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀_æ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¯û␀␀Ûû␀␀Úû␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀é4␀␀␀Ûû␀␀¯û␀␀Úû␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀_≤␀␀␀␀␀␀¯û␀␀Ûû␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀é4¯û␀␀␀␀␀Ûû␀␀¯û␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀é4␀␀␀Úû␀␀¯û␀␀Ûû␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀é4␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¯û␀␀Ûû␀␀Úû␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀é4␀␀␀'␀␀␀␀␀Ûû␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀_æ¯û␀␀Úû␀␀Ûû␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀é4¯û␀␀Ûû␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀é4␀␀␀␀␀¯û␀␀Úû␀␀Ûû␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀é4¯û␀␀Úû␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¯û␀␀Ûû␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀é4Ûû␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀_æÛû␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀é4Úû␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¯û␀␀␀␀␀Ω␀␀␀␀Item␀␀␀␀␀'␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Úû␀␀␀␀␀Ûû␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀lfß␀␀␀␀ÄºMode is no longer needed on input module as it handles both mouse an