er support.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¨The degree of darkness added by ambient occlusion. Higher values produce darker areas.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄêThe custom color to use for the ambient occlusion. The default is black.¨û␀␀␀␀Å–Check this box to mark this Volume as to only affect ambient lighting. This mode is only available with the Deferred rendering path and HDR rendering. Objects rendered with the Forward rendering path won't get any ambient occlusion.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä˙This modifies the thickness of occluders. It increases the size of dark areas and also introduces a dark halo around objects.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä∞Add a bias distance to sampled depth in AO to reduce self-shadowing aliasing artifacts. ¨û␀␀␀␀~Modifies the influence of direct lighting on ambient occlusion.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄäThe radius of sample points. This affects the size of darkened areas.¨û␀␀␀␀Å‹The number of sample points. This affects both quality and performance. For "Lowest", "Low", and "Medium", passes are downsampled. For "High" and "Ultra", they are not and therefore you should only "High" and "Ultra" on high-end hardware.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅöFilters the bright and dark parts of the histogram when computing the average luminance. This is to avoid very dark pixels and very bright pixels from contributing to the auto exposure. Unit is in percent.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄàMinimum average luminance to consider for auto exposure. Unit is EV.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄàMaximum average luminance to consider for auto exposure. Unit is EV.¨û␀␀␀␀fUse this to scale the global exposure of the scene.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä§Use "Progressive" if you want auto exposure to be animated. Use "Fixed" otherwise.¨û␀␀␀␀hAdaptation speed from a dark to a light environment.¨û␀␀␀␀hAdaptation speed from a light to a dark environment.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä‹Strength of the bloom filter. Values higher than 1 will make bloom contribute more energy to the final render.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄñFilters out pixels under this level of brightness. Value is in gamma-space.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä–Makes transitions between under/over-threshold gradual. 0 for a hard threshold, 1 for a soft threshold).¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÜClamps pixels to control the bloom amount. Value is in gamma-space.¨û␀␀␀␀Å¥Changes the extent of veiling effects. For maximum quality, use integer values. Because this value changes the internal iteration count, You should not animating it as it may introduce issues with the perceived radius.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄDistorts the bloom to give an anamorphic look. Negative values distort vertically, positive values distort horizontally.¨û␀␀␀␀@Global tint of the bloom filter.'¢␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀mobileOptimized␀␀ÅÇBoost performance by lowering the effect quality. This settings is meant to be used on mobile and other low-end platforms but can also provide a nice performance boost on desktops and consoles.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄåThe lens dirt texture used to add smudges or dust to the bloom effect.¨û␀␀␀␀HThe intensity of the lens dirtiness.¨û␀␀␀␀PShifts the hue of chromatic aberrations.¨û␀␀␀␀@Amount of tangential distortion.'¢␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀mobileOptimized␀␀ÅÑBoost performances by lowering the effect quality. This settings is meant to be used on mobile and other low-end platforms but can also provide a nice performance boost on desktops and consoles.¨û␀␀␀␀ÇDSelect a color grading mode that fits your dynamic range and workflow. Use HDR if your camera is set to render in HDR and your target platform supports it. Use LDR for low-end mobiles or devices that don't support HDR. Use External if you prefer authoring a Log LUT in an external software.¨û␀␀␀␀@A custom 3D log-encoded texture.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄúSelect a tonemapping algorithm to use at the end of the color grading process.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅAffects the transition between the toe and the mid section of the curve. A value of 0 means no toe, a value of 1 means a very hard transition.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅÄAffects how much of the dynamic range is in the toe. With a small value, the toe will be very short and quickly transition into the linear section, with a larger value, the toe will be longer.¨û␀␀␀␀Å0Affects the transition between the mid section and the shoulder of the curve. A value of 0 means no shoulder, a value of 1 means a very hard transition.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄéAffects how many F-stops (EV) to add to the dynamic range of the curve.¨û␀␀␀␀dAffects how much overshoot to add to the shoulder.¨û␀␀␀␀LApplies a gamma function to the curve.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅdCustom lookup texture (strip format, for example 256x16) to apply before the rest of the color grading operators. If none is provided, a neutral one will be generated internally.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄñHow much of the lookup texture will contribute to the color grading effect.¨û␀␀␀␀jSets the white balance to a custom color temperature.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÇSets the white balance to compensate for a green or magenta tint.¨û␀␀␀␀NTint the render by multiplying a color.¨û␀␀␀␀8Shift the hue of all colors.¨û␀␀␀␀FPushes the intensity of all colors.¨û␀␀␀␀FMakes the image brighter or darker.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅTAdjusts the overall exposure of the scene in EV units. This is applied after the HDR effect and right before tonemapping so it won't affect previous effects in the chain.¨û␀␀␀␀jExpands or shrinks the overall range of tonal values.¨û␀␀␀␀nModify influence of the red channel in the overall mix.¨û␀␀␀␀rModify influence of the green channel in the overall mix.¨û␀␀␀␀pModify influence of the blue channel in the overall mix.¨û␀␀␀␀nModify influence of the red channel in the overall mix.¨û␀␀␀␀rModify influence of the green channel in the overall mix.¨û␀␀␀␀pModify influence of the blue channel in the overall mix.¨û␀␀␀␀nModify influence of the red channel in the overall mix.¨û␀␀␀␀rModify influence of the green channel in the overall mix.¨û␀␀␀␀pModify influence of the blue channel in the overall mix.¨û␀␀␀␀XControls the darkest portions of the render.¨û␀␀␀␀ZPower function that controls mid-range tones.¨û␀␀␀␀ZControls the lightest portions of the render.¨û␀␀␀␀>Distance to the point of focus.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄﬁRatio of aperture (known as f-stop or f-number). The smaller the value is, the shallower the depth of field is.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä Distance between the lens and the film. The larger the value is, the shallower the depth of field is.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅpConvolution kernel size of the bokeh filter, which determines the maximum radius of bokeh. It also affects performances (the larger the kernel is, the longer the GPU time is required).'¢␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀mobileOptimized␀␀ÅÇBoost performances by lowering the effect quality. This setting is meant to be used on mobile and other low-end platforms but can also provide a nice performance boost on desktops and consoles.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅÜKeep alpha channel. This will slightly lower the effect quality but allows rendering against a transparent background.
This setting has no effect if the camera render target has no alpha channel.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¿Enables the internal deferred fog pass. Actual fog settings should be set in the Lighting panel.¨û␀␀␀␀TMark true for the fog to ignore the skybox¨û␀␀␀␀@Enable the use of colored grain.¨û␀␀␀␀lGrain strength. Higher values mean more visible grain.¨û␀␀␀␀(Grain particle size.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄŒControls the noise response curve based on scene luminance. Lower values mean less noise in dark areas.¨û␀␀␀␀0Total distortion amount.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¶Intensity multiplier on the x-axis. Set it to 0 to disable distortion on this axis.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä¶Intensity multiplier on the y-axis. Set it to 0 to disable distortion on this axis.¨û␀␀␀␀BDistortion center point (x-axis).¨û␀␀␀␀BDistortion center point (y-axis).¨û␀␀␀␀,Global screen scaling.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÄThe angle of rotary shutter. Larger values give longer exposure.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÑThe amount of sample points. This affects quality and performance.¨û␀␀␀␀Å<Choose a quality preset, or use "Custom" to create your own custom preset. Don't use a preset higher than "Medium" if you desire good performance on consoles.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä™Maximum number of steps in the raymarching pass. Higher values mean more reflections.¨û␀␀␀␀Å"Changes the size of the SSR buffer. Downsample it to maximize performances or supersample it for higher quality results with reduced performance.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄºRay thickness. Lower values are more expensive but allow the effect to detect smaller details.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄîMaximum distance to traverse after which it will stop drawing reflections.¨û␀␀␀␀VFades reflections close to the near planes.¨û␀␀␀␀XFades reflections close to the screen edges.¨û␀␀␀␀Ä™Lower quality is faster at the expense of visual quality (Low = ~60%, Medium = ~80%).¨û␀␀␀␀ÅvThe diameter (in texels) inside which jitter samples are spread. Smaller values result in crisper but more aliased output, while larger values result in more stable, but blurrier, output.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄﬁControls the amount of sharpening applied to the color buffer. High values may introduce dark-border artifacts.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄThe blend coefficient for a stationary fragment. Controls the percentage of history sample blended into the final color.¨û␀␀␀␀Å
The blend coefficient for a fragment with significant motion. Controls the percentage of history sample blended into the final color.␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀Ä∆Use the "Classic" mode for parametric controls. Use the "Masked" mode to use your own texture mask.¨û␀␀␀␀Vignette color.¨û␀␀␀␀zSets the vignette center point (screen center is [0.5, 0.5]).¨û␀␀␀␀>Amount of vignetting on screen.¨û␀␀␀␀FSmoothness of the vignette borders.¨û␀␀␀␀ZLower values will make a square-ish vignette.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄSet to true to mark the vignette to be perfectly round. False will make its shape dependent on the current aspect ratio.¨û␀␀␀␀XA black and white mask to use as a vignette.¨û␀␀␀␀Mask opacity.␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀á†␀␀␀␀␀8¢␀␀á†␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀8¢␀␀á†␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀á†␀␀␀␀␀á†␀␀␀␀␀á†␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀pA list of all settings currently stored in this profile.␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¨û␀␀␀␀ÄŒCheck this box to mark this volume as global. This volume's Profile will be applied to the whole Scene.¨û␀␀␀␀ÅéThe distance (from the attached Collider) to start blending from. A value of 0 means there will be no blending and the Volume overrides will be applied immediatly upon entry to the attached Collider.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÙThe total weight of this Volume in the Scene. A value of 0 signifies that it will have no effect, 1 signifies full effect.¨û␀␀␀␀ÄÃThe volume priority in the stack. A higher value means higher priority. Negative values are supported.␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀á†␀␀␀␀␀á†␀␀␀␀␀á†␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀